      "writtenOn": "2025-02-21T00:25:28Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I am not sure I understand your comment but let me clarify some behavior.\n\nBLE_ON is an intermediary state between ON and OFF, whatever the end state is, there will be a passage via BLE_ON.\n\nBut, while ACTION_BLE_STATE_CHANGED will send all the transition that are describ in your doc, the ACTION_STATE_CHANGED will only sent the br-edr one and that mean that for a full lifecycle you will have\n\n```\nOFF -\u003e BLE_TURNING_ON : Only send ACTION_BLE_STATE_CHANGED\nBLE_TURNING_ON -\u003e BLE_ON : Only send ACTION_BLE_STATE_CHANGED\nBLE_ON -\u003e TURNING_ON : send both ACTION_BLE_STATE_CHANGED and ACTION_STATE_CHANGED, but the prev state in ACTION_STATE_CHANGED is OFF and not BLE_ON\nTURNING_ON -\u003e ON : send both ACTION_BLE_STATE_CHANGED and ACTION_STATE_CHANGED \nON -\u003e TURNING_OFF : send both ACTION_BLE_STATE_CHANGED and ACTION_STATE_CHANGED\nTURNING_OFF-\u003e BLE_ON: send both ACTION_BLE_STATE_CHANGED and ACTION_STATE_CHANGED but the new state in ACTION_STATE_CHANGED is OFF\nBLE_ON -\u003e BLE_TURNING_OFF : Only send ACTION_BLE_STATE_CHANGED\nBLE_TURNING_OFF -\u003e OFF : Only send ACTION_BLE_STATE_CHANGED\n```\n\nKnowing that, and knowing that you are interested in BLE_ON state, I would listen to BLE_ON on ACTION_BLE_STATE_CHANGED and disregard the ACTION_STATE_CHANGED.\n\nThen, if you want to make sure that BLE_ON is not just a passage in your case, this check:\n```\nwaittingBluetoothState(BluetoothAdapter.STATE_OFF)\nTruth.assertThat(mAdapter.leState).isEqualTo(BluetoothAdapter.STATE_BLE_ON)\n```\n\ndoes not do that, as you will have STATE_OFF even when being in BLE_ON and even if this is a transitionary state. Let\u0027s imagine that bluetooth will end up shutting down, you could call mAdapter.leState before it goes to BLE_TURNING_OFF.\n\nI think that, what I would do is to call \n```\nwaittingBluetoothState(BluetoothAdapter.STATE_BLE_ON)\n\nassertThrows(IOException::class.java) { socketOs.write(data) }  // see my other comment\n\n// Validate that the test run while BLuetooth stayed in BLE_ON all along\nTruth.assertThat(mAdapter.leState).isEqualTo(BluetoothAdapter.STATE_BLE_ON)\n```\n\nThat way, you proceed the test when you reach BLE_ON, somebody reading this test later will know that you intended BLE_ON (and not OFF) and you can still assert the BLE_ON state stayed during the whole test",
      "parentUuid": "006f5bd3_54b7694d",
